spans.s:
नि-वार, अस्, m. keeping off, warding off, defend-
ing, preventing, hindering, opposition, impediment;
(आ), f., N. of a river.

नीवर नीवर, अस्, m. (said to be fr. rt. 1.
नी), trade, traffic; a trader; a religious mendicant;
a place suitable for the site of a habitation; mud;
(अम्), n. water.

नीवार नीवार, अस्, m. rice growing wild or
without cultivation; (आस्), m. pl. grains of wild rice;
(आ), f., N. of a river.

निवस् 1. नि-वस्, cl. 1. P. -वसति, -वस्तुम्,
to dwell, inhabit, reside, lodge, live in (with loc.);
to sojourn, pass time, pass the night; to roost.

2. नि-वसन, अम्, n. (for 1. see under 1. नि-वस्,
col. 1), putting on (a garment), cloth, clothes, raiment;
an under garment.

1. नि-वासन, अम्, n. (for 2. see under 2. नि-वस्
below), living, residing [cf. जगन्-न्˚]; sojourn;
passing or spending time.

नि-वेदित, अस्, आ, अम्, made known, announced,
reported, communicated, stated, told; represented;
delivered, addressed, entrusted, presented, given.

नि-विष्ट, अस्, आ, अम्, entered, gone in or into;
seated, sitting upon; situated or placed in or on,
fixed on, intent upon; arranged.

नि-वेशित, अस्, आ, अम्, made to enter, introduced;
placed in or upon; turned to or towards, fixed upon,
directed on; sent into, entered into, engaged in.

नि-वृत्ति, इस्, f. returning, return; disappearing,
disappearance, ceasing, cessation, suspension, abstain-
ing from action, inactivity, (opposed to प्र-वृत्ति);
ceasing to be active, ceasing to be valid or binding
(as a rule); leaving off, desisting from (with abl.);
resigning, discontinuance of worldly acts, cessation of
emotions, separation from the world, abstinence;
repose, rest; felicity, bliss, beatitude, (wrongly for
निर्-वृत्ति); denial, refusal; abolition, prevention;
completion; (इस्), m., N. of a son of Vṛṣṇi; of a
son of Dharma, king of Magadha; [cf. निर्-वृत्ति,
नि-धृति.]

नि-वर्तनीय, अस्, आ, अम्, to be brought back;
to be made to turn back, to be kept back or diverted
or hindered.

नीवाक नीवाक, अस्, m. (fr. नि-वच्), the
increased demand for grain in times of dearth;
dearth, scarcity.
